--TEST--
A `#[Route]` handler's text outside `<?nvs … ?>` writes the response body as `echo` does, so it beside a `Core\Response` body member is two writers in either order, while page text and `echo` together are one -- `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`, `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/code-mode-and-html-mode
// Page text in a request is written raw by the HTML sink, the same sink `echo`
// writes to, so a JSON body with page text after it would be a JSON endpoint
// with an HTML tail.
class Pages
{
    #[Core\Route(path: "/report", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function report(): void
    {
        Core\Response::json(1);
?>
<p>footer</p>
<?nvs
    }

    // The other order is the same mistake.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/receipt", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function receipt(): void
    {
?>
<h1>Receipt</h1>
<?nvs
        Core\Response::text("receipt");
    }

    // The accepted half: page text, a short echo tag and `echo` all write one
    // HTML body, so together they are one writer.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/page", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function page(): void
    {
        string $title = "Page";
?>
<h1><?= $title ?></h1>
<?nvs
        echo "<p>one writer</p>";
    }
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0801]: a response has one body, and this handler writes it with both `Core\Response::json` and the HTML outside `<?nvs ?>`
%A
error[E0801]: a response has one body, and this handler writes it with both the HTML outside `<?nvs ?>` and `Core\Response::text`
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
